def markdown(text):
    return {"cell_type": "markdown", "metadata": {}, "source": text.splitlines(keepends=True)}


def code(text):
    return {"cell_type": "code", "execution_count": None, "metadata": {},
            "outputs": [], "source": text.splitlines(keepends=True)}


cells = [
    markdown("# TF-IDF Final: NLP lost-item candidate ranking\n\nThis notebook demonstrates a reproducible **fictional** Strathmore-themed exercise. A lost report is compared with a pool of found reports and the system returns candidates for staff review. A score does not verify ownership or authorize release. The 2026 office inventories supply aggregate category counts only; none of the individual records below came from those inventories."),
    markdown("## 1. Sources and dataset\n\nThe user transcribed two Office category tables. Their quantities total 388 for June–July and 299 for August. The linked original Google Docs were inaccessible to the connected account, so the transcription still needs checking against them. The controlled lost and found descriptions below were AI authored as fictional development examples; independent team review is pending."),
    code("from pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nimport csv, json\nROOT = Path.cwd()\nassert (ROOT / 'scripts/prepare_ranking_benchmark.py').exists(), 'Run from repository root'\nfrom scripts.prepare_ranking_benchmark import main as prepare\nfrom scripts.validate_ranking_dataset import validate\n_NOTEBOOK_TMP = TemporaryDirectory(prefix='nlp-ranking-')\nRUN_DIR = Path(_NOTEBOOK_TMP.name)\nDATA = RUN_DIR / 'ranking'\nprepare(DATA)\naudit = validate(DATA)\nassert audit['valid'], audit['errors']\nprint(json.dumps(audit, indent=2))"),
    markdown("## 2. Preparation and leakage control\n\nEach controlled item has one found record and a separately worded lost claim. Labels use the controlled item-group ID. Training, validation and test groups are disjoint; no pair is randomly split. No-match queries have no relevant found ID. The fixed candidate pool contains all 24 fictional found records."),
    code("def read_csv(name):\n    with (DATA / name).open(encoding='utf-8', newline='') as stream:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(stream))\nfound = read_csv('found_reports.csv'); lost = read_csv('lost_reports.csv')\nlabels = read_csv('match_labels.csv'); queries = read_csv('evaluation_queries.csv')\nfor split in ('train', 'validation', 'test'):\n    groups = {r['item_group_id'] for r in found if r['split'] == split}\n    print(split, 'item groups:', len(groups), 'matched queries:', sum(q['split']==split and q['has_match']=='1' for q in queries))\nprint('sources:', sorted({r['source_type'] for r in found}))\nprint('example:', {k: found[0][k] for k in ('found_id','category','description','source_type')})"),
    markdown("## 3. Logistic Regression comparison\n\nThe earlier CAT 2 coursework used Logistic Regression on engineered text and attribute comparisons. This benchmark fits a fresh Logistic Regression model on its 64 training pairs; it does not load the older notebook's model or artifact. Its scores rank the same candidate pool as the retrieval methods and remain results on constructed labels."),
    code("from matching_system.feature_builder import FEATURE_NAMES, pair_features\nfrom scripts.evaluate_ranking_benchmark import as_report\ntrain_labels = [r for r in labels if r['split']=='train']\nfound_by_id = {r['found_id']: r for r in found}; lost_by_id = {r['lost_id']: r for r in lost}\nexample = train_labels[0]\nfeatures = pair_features(as_report(lost_by_id[example['lost_id']], 'lost'), as_report(found_by_id[example['found_id']], 'found'))\nprint('training pairs:', len(train_labels), 'features:', list(features))"),
    markdown("## 4. NLP text features and cosine similarity\n\nTF-IDF gives more weight to terms that distinguish documents in the fitted corpus. Unigrams and bigrams retain individual words and short phrases. The vocabulary below is fitted only on training descriptions and then reused to transform validation, test and candidate records. Cosine similarity measures the angle between their feature vectors; it is textual relevance, not a probability of ownership."),
    code("from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nfrom matching_system.nlp_retrieval import normalize_report_text\ntraining_text = [normalize_report_text(r['description']) for r in found + lost if r['split']=='train']\nvectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(ngram_range=(1,2), strip_accents='unicode').fit(training_text)\nquery_text = normalize_report_text(lost_by_id['l-g017']['description'])\nfound_text = normalize_report_text(found_by_id['f-g017']['description'])\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vectorizer.transform([query_text]), vectorizer.transform([found_text]))[0,0]\nprint('vocabulary size:', len(vectorizer.vocabulary_), 'example cosine:', round(float(similarity), 4))"),
    markdown("## 5. Hybrid ranking and validation\n\nThe comparison script applies one candidate pool to keyword overlap, attribute scoring, Logistic Regression, TF-IDF cosine and a weighted hybrid. It selects a hybrid weight from 0.25, 0.50 or 0.75 using validation Recall@5 and MRR. A strong-suggestion cutoff is also derived from validation no-match scores. The test split is used only for reporting."),
    code("from scripts.evaluate_ranking_benchmark import main as evaluate\nresults = evaluate(data_dir=DATA, result_path=RUN_DIR/'ranking_benchmark.json')\nprint('selected TF-IDF weight:', results['hybrid']['tfidf_weight_selected_on_validation'])\nprint('training feature count:', len(results['logistic_regression']['features']))"),
    markdown("## 6. Held-out ranking results\n\nTop-1 accuracy asks whether the relevant found record is first. Recall@5 asks whether it appears in the first five. MRR averages the reciprocal of its first relevant rank. False suggestions count no-match queries whose top score exceeds the validation cutoff. All denominators are printed below. These are controlled-fiction results, not campus recovery rates."),
    code("print('method | top1 | recall@5 | MRR | no-match false suggestions')\nfor name, item in results['methods'].items():\n    test = item['test']\n    print(f\"{name} | {test['top1_accuracy']:.3f} ({test['matched_queries']}) | {test['recall_at_5']:.3f} | {test['mrr']:.3f} | {item['test_no_match_false_suggestions']}/{item['test_no_match_denominator']}\")"),
    markdown("## 7. Explainability and application integration\n\nThe runtime `MatchingPipeline` returns lexical similarity alongside structured fact statuses and staff-workflow guidance. The cell below calls the FastAPI preview route function directly with a validated synthetic request. This checks its model-to-explanation path but does not test HTTP routing, the authenticated Firebase service, or Android connectivity. Ordinary users must not receive private verification details or treat recommendations as release decisions."),
    code("from pydantic import ValidationError\nfrom matching_system.api import PreviewRequest, preview_matches\nrequest = PreviewRequest(item_name='Metal water bottle',description='Blue bottle with a scratched lid',category_id='metal_water_bottle',location_id='msb',event_date='2026-11-10',limit=3)\nbody = preview_matches(request)\nprint('status:', body['status'], 'first ID:', body['matches'][0]['found_report_id'])\nprint('explanation:', body['matches'][0]['explanation'])\ntry:\n    PreviewRequest(item_name='', description='')\nexcept ValidationError:\n    print('blank input: rejected')"),
    markdown("## 8. Interpretation and limits\n\nThe structured and hybrid methods score perfectly on the eight matched fictional test queries because the generated lost and found records share category and colour fields by construction. That is a benchmark design weakness, not evidence of field effectiveness. TF-IDF alone misses several paraphrases after fitting on only eight training groups. No-match false suggestions show that a nonempty pool still needs cautious review. A new independently authored and reviewed set of item identities, with a frozen holdout, is required before a real deployment or generalization claim. The team must check the source inventory documents, review labels, verify the authenticated service and Android flow, and approve this notebook for submission."),
]

